\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\keepn\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 \page }{\cs16\lang2057\langfe3082\super\langnp2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain 
\s15\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1027\langfe3082\cgrid\langnp1027\langfenp3082 {\cs16\lang2057\langfe3082\super\langnp2057 #}{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057  COPIA}}+{\footnote 
\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1027\langfe3082\cgrid\langnp1027\langfenp3082 {\cs16\lang2057\langfe3082\super\langnp2057 +}{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057  CF:0050}}$
{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1027\langfe3082\cgrid\langnp1027\langfenp3082 {\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 $ Copying normal packs (PAC)}}}{
\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf2\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 Copying normal packs (PAC)
\par }{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 
\par One of the functions of the programme is to copy all the elements referenced by a normal Clic pack (files with the extension PAC) to a different folder. This operation allows files that ar
e not used in any activity to be found, thus freeing space in the hard disc.
\par 
\par The steps to follow for copying a pack are:
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \fi-284\li284\ri0\nowidctlpar\tx284\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin284\itap0 {\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 \{bmc bola.bmp\}\tab Choose the kind of }{\b\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 Normal Clic pack (PAC) }{
\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 file.
\par 
\par \{bmc bola.bmp\}\tab Choose the unit and the directory where the pack is found.
\par 
\par \{bmc bola.bmp\}\tab Click on the name of the pack to be copied. If it is a group of connected packs choose the main one.
\par 
\par \{bmc bola.bmp\}\tab When the process of analysing its contents has finished, click on }{\b\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 Copy}{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057  button.
\par 
\par \{bmc bola.bmp\}\tab Choose the unit and th
e directory where you want to copy the files. It must be different to the one where the pack was originally. We suggest you use a clean directory, where there is no other file. You cannot create other directories from ClicPac, but they can be made using t
he }{\i\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 File Manager }{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 of Windows 3.1, or the }{\i\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 Explorer }{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 of Windows 95.
\par 
\par \{bmc bola.bmp\}\tab Choose the files to be copied (it's advisable to leave them all marked), and activate the }{\b\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 Copy}{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057  button.
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 
\par After copying a file to a new destination we recommend testing it. If all goes well you can eliminate the contents of the original directory.
\par 
\par \{bmc linia.bmp\}
\par 
\par }{\uldb\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 \{bmc back.bmp\}}{\v\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 INDEX}{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057    Back to contents
\par 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\keepn\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 \page }{\cs16\lang2057\langfe3082\super\langnp2057 #{\footnote \pard\plain 
\s15\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1027\langfe3082\cgrid\langnp1027\langfenp3082 {\cs16\lang2057\langfe3082\super\langnp2057 #}{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057  DIFE}}+{\footnote 
\pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1027\langfe3082\cgrid\langnp1027\langfenp3082 {\cs16\lang2057\langfe3082\super\langnp2057 +}{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057  CF:0060}}$
{\footnote \pard\plain \s15\ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1027\langfe3082\cgrid\langnp1027\langfenp3082 {\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 $ Differences between PAC and PCC packs}}}{
\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf2\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 Differences between PAC and PCC packs
\par }{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 
\par }\pard \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\hyphpar0\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 
\par The compact Clic packs (files with the extension PCC) can group all the material necessary for executing an activity pack in one file, with some exceptions:
\par 
\par The multimedia files (WAV, AVI, MIDI, etc.) must be located outside the compact pack so that Windows can interpret them.
\par 
\par The auxiliary files (WRI, DLL, EXE, etc.) must also keep their independent format.
\par 
\par It is not obligatory to include all the material of a pack in the PCC file. If you see fit, you can leave some files in their original independent format. When Clic uses a compact pack it tries to find the material necessary for exec
uting the activities in the PCC file. If it doesn't find them it will look outside, and if they still don't appear it will show an error message.
\par 
\par The activities and packs cannot be modified when they are inside a PCC pack. If you want to make any modifica
tions, or study their contents, you have to decompact them and open them again in their original PAC format. Once they have been modified we suggest that you compact them again.
\par 
\par If you have compacted a group of linked packs, the pack that will run when you load the PCC file will always be the one that was chosen as the main pack in the moment of compacting.
\par 
\par The PCC files don\rquote t compress data (as would a ZIP), but limit themselves to connecting the information corresponding to the different that they contai
n. Nevertheless, the use of PCC compact packs allows you to save disc space, as they considerably reduce the residual occupation caused by the storage system by means of accommodation tables (FAT), which is used in systems based on MS-DOS and Windows 95.

\par 
\par Clic activities usually use a lot of small files. The organisation of the hard disc in FAT means that each of these files occupies, in reality, a bigger space than it really needs. This space may reach, in the bigger discs, a minimum of 30kb per file. The
 
hard discs are organised in clusters, which are the rooms where the files are kept. Each file occupies at least one cluster (they cannot be shared, even though we're dealing with small files). As a result a file with a single line of text that theoretical
ly occupies 60 characters, is in reality occupying 30,000 in the hard disc. The use of PCC packs can spectacularly reduce this residual loss of space.
\par 
\par \{bmc linia.bmp\}
\par 
\par }{\uldb\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 \{bmc back.bmp\}}{\v\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057 INDEX}{\lang2057\langfe3082\langnp2057    Back to contents
